WEBVTT

STYLE
::cue(c.color9CD5FF) { color: #9CD5FF; }

1
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:07.000 line:5% align:center
<b><c.color9CD5FF>One Pace Addon</c></b>

2
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:52.710
[COLLECTOR MAN A]
Hold it right there, Paulie!

3
00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:54.810
You're not getting away this time!

4
00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:56.650
I'm telling you,
I'll get you the money,

5
00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:58.820
you just have to
wait a little longer!

6
00:01:58.850 --> 00:01:59.750
[ICEBURG]
Is that?

7
00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:01.450
[KALIFA]
Yes, it's Paulie.

8
00:02:01.490 --> 00:02:02.630
The debt collectors are after him again.

9
00:02:02.630 --> 00:02:04.600
[COLLECTOR MAN A]
Give us the money!

10
00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:05.530
For the last time,

11
00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:07.500
I can't give you
money if I don't have it!

12
00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:10.800
[COLLECTOR MAN B]
Stop making excuses and pay up!

13
00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:13.540
You're gonna chase me all the way to my
grave, aren't you?

14
00:02:13.570 --> 00:02:15.910
Yagaras, and just in time, too!

15
00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:18.880
Rope...

16
00:02:18.910 --> 00:02:22.780
...Action!

17
00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:23.680
Ahhh!

18
00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:24.030
Hey, that's one of

19
00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:25.200
the shipwrights from
Galley-La, ain't it?

20
00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:28.850
[PAULIE]
Sorry fellas, but
I gotta borrow those Yagaras!

21
00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:31.020
Now! Time for my Round Turn!

22
00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:32.520
Hyah!

23
00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:38.590
[loud bam]

24
00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:47.840
[chuckles]

25
00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:52.270
<b>The Case of the Kidnapped Pirate</b>

26
00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:56.480
You punk!

27
00:02:56.510 --> 00:02:58.180
Just wait!
We'll get you next time!

28
00:02:58.210 --> 00:03:01.020
So long, gentlemen, it was
a wonderful race, as always.

29
00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:04.720
Take care and maybe you
can try again next week.

30
00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:05.620
Damn it!

31
00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:11.500
<b>Paulie
Rigging and Mast Foreman
Dock One
Galley-La Company</b>

32
00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:07.750
[PAULIE]
I gotta admit, for a Yagara,

33
00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:10.520
you sure have a good
sense of dramatic timing.

34
00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:15.660
That was close!

35
00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:18.160
Those shipwrights of yours have
got some pretty fancy moves!

36
00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:19.520
That they do.

37
00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:21.560
Hey buddy, thanks for the help!

38
00:03:21.590 --> 00:03:24.660
I was starting to think we'd
never see our money again!

39
00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:26.390
Money?

40
00:03:26.570 --> 00:03:28.070
Oh... He must mean this.

41
00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:34.580
[USOPP]
Helloooooooo...?

42
00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:35.580
You gotta be kidding me.

43
00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:37.020
Hey! Come back here!

44
00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:38.790
I'll take care of this!

45
00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:40.560
Jerks stealing our stuff.

46
00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:44.400
I'll get your money back.

47
00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:47.660
Huh?! Wait, a pigeon?

48
00:03:48.050 --> 00:03:50.020
[PAULIE]
Ow, ow, ow, ow, ow!

49
00:03:50.050 --> 00:03:53.260
Okay, lemme go! Lemme go!
Take it easy! Jeez!

50
00:03:53.290 --> 00:03:54.320
I'm not gonna run away!

51
00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:57.230
You got me!
You win, all right?!

52
00:03:57.260 --> 00:03:59.200
You can stop pinching my ear now!

53
00:03:58.630 --> 00:04:04.930
<b>Rob Lucci
Sawyer and Treenail Foreman
Dock One
Galley-La Company</b>

54
00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:00.400
Ow!

55
00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:02.930
I was lucky enough to find that money and
you screw it up!

56
00:04:02.980 --> 00:04:05.220
Better watch your back
Lucci, I'll remember this!

57
00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:08.020
Don't pay back your debt with
someone else's money, idiot.

58
00:04:08.020 --> 00:04:09.050
Have you no shame?

59
00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:11.650
Hey, I happened upon that money fair and
square!

60
00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:14.460
[HATTORI]
Stealing from thieves
is still stealing! Coo coo!

61
00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:15.460
[PAULIE]
I didn't steal anything.

62
00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:18.100
I was just borrowing
their Yagara, that's all!

63
00:04:18.140 --> 00:04:19.940
[ICEBURG]
Looks like they're back.

64
00:04:19.970 --> 00:04:23.280
That's great! I'm glad things
worked out for you, Usopp!

65
00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:24.580
Worked out for me?

66
00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:27.630
That 200 million was for
ship repairs, remember?

67
00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:30.130
That's right-and all
you did was sit there

68
00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:32.240
and stare like a fool while
it nearly got carried away!

69
00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:35.150
I know but that pigeon
said he'd handle it.

70
00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:37.730
Mmwell, that was embarrassing.

71
00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:40.700
Rest assured, we will deal
with our employee appropriately.

72
00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:42.100
Allow us to handle this,

73
00:04:42.170 --> 00:04:43.740
so there's no bad
blood between you.

74
00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:45.710
Although Paulie's behavior
was reprehensible,

75
00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:47.680
it's fortunate that
he saved your money

76
00:04:47.710 --> 00:04:48.640
from the Franky Family.

77
00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:50.610
Well. I guess you could
look at it that way.

78
00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:54.510
No harm done, right?

79
00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:56.510
So, who is this Franky Family?

80
00:04:56.540 --> 00:04:59.380
Do they rob from everyone
they run into or just us?

81
00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:02.620
Well, it depends, but normally,
they dismantle ships.

82
00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:03.750
You mean they're workmen?

83
00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:04.780
I don't know about you,

84
00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:07.520
but I'd never hire a
team that looks like that.

85
00:05:07.570 --> 00:05:10.200
Indeed. But they're
not just ship dismantlers,

86
00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:11.540
they're also bounty hunters.

87
00:05:11.570 --> 00:05:13.710
Every time they find out
there are pirates in town,

88
00:05:13.790 --> 00:05:15.290
they cause quite a ruckus.

89
00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:17.830
To put it simply, they're just a public
nuisance.

90
00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:19.380
[ICEBURG]
Not only do
they collect bounties

91
00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:20.790
on the pirates they defeat,

92
00:05:20.830 --> 00:05:23.170
they also take
possession of their ships.

93
00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:24.670
Afterwards,
they dismantle them

94
00:05:24.700 --> 00:05:27.240
and sell whatever parts
they can on the open market.

95
00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:30.210
It may seem underhanded, but for them,
it's good business.

96
00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:31.500
What a bunch of jerkwads!

97
00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:32.860
It's like they're
trying to dismantle

98
00:05:32.890 --> 00:05:34.430
the whole pirate lifestyle!

99
00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:35.600
Mmwell. If they catch you,

100
00:05:35.630 --> 00:05:37.330
they will certainly
bleed you dry.

101
00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:40.370
I dunno, those guys looked
too weak to do anything to us.

102
00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:42.800
The men you saw were underlings.

103
00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:46.280
Franky, the leader of the group,
is not nearly as harmless.

104
00:05:46.310 --> 00:05:50.810
If I were you, I would avoid him at any
cost.

105
00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:54.130
Here you go, Iceburg.
Do with him as you will.

106
00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:55.960
I'm here.
You can let go!

107
00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:58.100
Excellent work as always, Lucci.

108
00:05:57.790 --> 00:06:01.420
<b>Hattori
Lucci's Pigeon</b>

109
00:05:58.130 --> 00:05:59.870
Truly. I can't apologize enough

110
00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:01.700
for the actions of
this oaf. Coo-Coo!

111
00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:03.000
Ah! It is talking!

112
00:06:03.740 --> 00:06:04.770
Coo coo!

113
00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:08.710
Tell the nice people that you're sorry,
oaf!

114
00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:12.180
That pigeon really has it
out for that dude, doesn't he?

115
00:06:12.220 --> 00:06:15.020
So the little bird talks, but the guy in
the hat doesn't?

116
00:06:15.050 --> 00:06:18.220
Anyway, I'm just glad
we got all our money back.

117
00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:21.020
[PAULIE]
Oh, so these
belong to you, huh?

118
00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:22.360
Good thing I was around.

119
00:06:22.390 --> 00:06:23.960
Yeah! Thanks a lot!

120
00:06:24.140 --> 00:06:26.240
If you wanna
thank me, gimme 10%.

121
00:06:26.270 --> 00:06:27.270
[bonk]

122
00:06:29.270 --> 00:06:31.340
Again. I'm quite sorry.

123
00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:34.580
This imbecile is addicted to gambling,
you see.

124
00:06:34.580 --> 00:06:36.110
He's racked up
such a massive debt

125
00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:38.320
that it's made him lose
sight of everything but greed.

126
00:06:38.350 --> 00:06:39.380
I don't care about him,

127
00:06:39.410 --> 00:06:41.350
I wanna know why
you're talking!

128
00:06:41.350 --> 00:06:42.030
[PAULIE]
I've had about enough

129
00:06:42.030 --> 00:06:44.300
of you running
your mouth, Lucci.

130
00:06:44.300 --> 00:06:46.070
This'll shut you up.

131
00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:48.060
Rope Action!

132
00:06:48.830 --> 00:06:51.030
Coocalookakoo!

133
00:06:51.830 --> 00:06:54.100
Bowline Knot!

134
00:06:57.870 --> 00:06:59.200
Punishment!

135
00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:05.110
Oh no! He's serious, isn't he?

136
00:07:07.910 --> 00:07:09.950
Hook, line, and sinker!

137
00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:15.330
Is he okay?

138
00:07:15.870 --> 00:07:18.200
Mmwell. This is nothing new.

139
00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:23.840
Huh? Whoa! Check it out!

140
00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:25.010
I thought he
landed on his head,

141
00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:28.950
but he stopped
himself with his arm!

142
00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:30.960
Wow. Not only that,

143
00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:33.230
but he dug his hand
into the ground!

144
00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:35.900
What, does he have fingers of steel or
something?

145
00:07:36.990 --> 00:07:37.960
These guys are
just shipwrights!

146
00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:40.990
What do they need all
these crazy powers for?!

147
00:07:42.290 --> 00:07:44.150
Hey, you with the goggles on your head!

148
00:07:44.150 --> 00:07:46.110
Huh?

149
00:07:46.270 --> 00:07:48.880
Listen man, think
about this for a second!

150
00:07:48.980 --> 00:07:52.650
That guy wasn't the one saying
all that mean stuff. It was...

151
00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:54.120
...the pigeon!

152
00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:01.080
The bird can say whatever it wants!

153
00:08:01.110 --> 00:08:04.330
Actions speak louder than words,
and Lucci's the one who hit me.

154
00:08:04.330 --> 00:08:06.070
Yeah, but the pigeon
made him hit you

155
00:08:06.100 --> 00:08:08.970
'cause he's too much of
a coward to do it himself!

156
00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:11.310
Hey, pigeon!

157
00:08:11.340 --> 00:08:13.880
Get down here and fight your own fights!

158
00:08:14.590 --> 00:08:16.180
Say something!

159
00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:22.950
Coo coo!

160
00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:24.590
Oh, you want some more?

161
00:08:24.620 --> 00:08:26.160
I'll take you on any time!

162
00:08:26.180 --> 00:08:27.850
Enough, Paulie.

163
00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:32.220
Coocoolookoo, please
excuse us for all the fuss!

164
00:08:32.260 --> 00:08:33.990
I'm Rob Lu...

165
00:08:34.020 --> 00:08:36.760
I mean, my name's Hattori.
And this is Rob Lucci.

166
00:08:36.940 --> 00:08:39.400
We both welcome you to Galley-La's factory.

167
00:08:39.430 --> 00:08:40.220
Hold on.

168
00:08:40.220 --> 00:08:42.480
The pigeon almost introduced
himself as that guy, didn't he?

169
00:08:45.450 --> 00:08:48.150
Oh! Cool! This is a
ventriloquism act, isn't it?!

170
00:08:48.150 --> 00:08:51.550
Really?! You were the coolest
trash talking pigeon ever!

171
00:08:51.570 --> 00:08:52.710
Even I couldn't tell it was you

172
00:08:52.740 --> 00:08:54.610
and I'm usually pretty sharp!

173
00:08:54.610 --> 00:08:57.680
No, please. It's really nothing
special at all. Coo coo!

174
00:08:57.720 --> 00:09:00.720
Hahahaha! He's just a
weirdo who doesn't know how

175
00:09:00.750 --> 00:09:01.950
to talk to people on his own.

176
00:09:01.990 --> 00:09:03.190
Hahahaha.

177
00:09:03.220 --> 00:09:04.890
Ahhh!

178
00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:05.820
Hold on a second,

179
00:09:05.860 --> 00:09:07.690
what does that woman
think she's doing?!

180
00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:10.150
Huh? What do you mean?

181
00:09:10.180 --> 00:09:12.020
Have you no shame?!

182
00:09:13.790 --> 00:09:15.460
[PAULIE]
Don't play dumb
with me, you harlot!

183
00:09:15.490 --> 00:09:18.430
I can see way too much leg!

184
00:09:18.460 --> 00:09:20.900
Cover up and show a
little decency, will ya?!

185
00:09:20.930 --> 00:09:23.010
This dock is a man's
workplace, you know!

186
00:09:23.010 --> 00:09:24.240
Now now, Paulie.

187
00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:26.070
[KALIFA]
Let's calm down.

188
00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:28.720
Ahhh!

189
00:09:31.050 --> 00:09:32.650
You too, Kalifa!

190
00:09:32.710 --> 00:09:33.680
How many times
have I told you

191
00:09:33.740 --> 00:09:36.010
those clothes of yours
are not appropriate?!

192
00:09:36.040 --> 00:09:37.170
If you want to
look professional

193
00:09:37.180 --> 00:09:39.890
you should wear pants or at
least ankle-length skirts!

194
00:09:39.890 --> 00:09:40.900
This guy's got issues.

195
00:09:40.950 --> 00:09:42.260
He's serious, isn't he?

196
00:09:42.310 --> 00:09:44.920
Hahahaha!

197
00:09:44.950 --> 00:09:47.050
[ICEBURG]
Mmwell enough of that.

198
00:09:47.080 --> 00:09:49.690
Let's open the gate and start the tour
already.

199
00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:02.470
That's just about the
biggest door I've ever seen.

200
00:10:02.500 --> 00:10:03.300
And whoa!

201
00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:06.000
You have to open
that thing by hand?!

202
00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:08.860
Wait those two can't do
that on their own, right?

203
00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:12.840
Wow, I guess they can!

204
00:10:12.940 --> 00:10:15.740
Mmwell, each dock
only has five foremen,

205
00:10:15.820 --> 00:10:17.090
and despite
their peculiarities,

206
00:10:17.130 --> 00:10:18.960
these two are both
talented enough

207
00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:21.280
to hold a position at Dock One.

208
00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:23.920
Kaku, who you met
earlier, is a foreman too.

209
00:10:23.950 --> 00:10:26.990
In a place like this, talent is the only
thing that matters.

210
00:10:27.020 --> 00:10:29.420
So even if they're a bit strange,
we don't mind.

211
00:10:29.460 --> 00:10:30.990
They are pretty weird, though.

212
00:10:31.020 --> 00:10:32.990
Ah! We can go in now!

213
00:10:33.080 --> 00:10:35.080
Remember to keep a close eye on your
money.

214
00:10:35.110 --> 00:10:37.870
Not that I'm saying we have thieves
inside, mind you.

215
00:10:37.870 --> 00:10:40.770
Yeah. I'm really sorry you
got dragged into that mess...

216
00:10:40.770 --> 00:10:42.240
I shoulda known better.

217
00:10:42.270 --> 00:10:43.910
Luffy, wait!

218
00:10:43.940 --> 00:10:46.680
Do a better job of protecting me next time
around!

219
00:10:46.710 --> 00:10:48.910
Okay, no problem! I got this!

220
00:10:48.930 --> 00:10:49.900
I'm worried.

221
00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:52.350
[ICEBURG]
Dock One employs
the strongest workers

222
00:10:52.390 --> 00:10:54.020
of the Galley-La Company.

223
00:10:54.050 --> 00:10:57.190
We take on our most difficult requests
here.

224
00:10:57.240 --> 00:10:58.980
Come on, let's take a look inside.

225
00:10:59.070 --> 00:11:00.300
[STRAW HAT CREW gasps]

226
00:11:03.220 --> 00:11:05.260
Whooooa!

227
00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:18.770
[LUFFY]
It's super huge!

228
00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:20.110
I knew it was
gonna be awesome,

229
00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:21.920
but it's even
better up close!

230
00:11:21.960 --> 00:11:22.920
No kidding!

231
00:11:22.960 --> 00:11:24.060
Yeah!

232
00:11:24.090 --> 00:11:25.130
[USOPP]
Ooh, look over there!

233
00:11:25.170 --> 00:11:26.800
They're building a big galleon!

234
00:11:26.840 --> 00:11:28.140
I wonder who it's for?

235
00:11:28.190 --> 00:11:30.500
[NAMI]
Wow, they've got a
full force working here.

236
00:11:30.530 --> 00:11:35.370
[ICEBURG]
Now, this way, if you will.

237
00:11:35.430 --> 00:11:37.900
Whoa, you guys see
that? Iceburg's here!

238
00:11:37.930 --> 00:11:41.030
Hey! Look sharp for
the boss, everyone!

239
00:11:41.070 --> 00:11:43.240
[CRAFTSMAN C]
Good morning Mr. Iceburg!

240
00:11:43.270 --> 00:11:45.170
Nice to see you here, Sir!

241
00:11:45.180 --> 00:11:46.140
[CRAFTSMAN E]
Hey Boss!

242
00:11:46.140 --> 00:11:48.220
Could you take a look at
the outer panels for me?

243
00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:50.760
Sure. I'll stop by later.

244
00:11:50.790 --> 00:11:52.830
[CRAFTSMAN F]
Lovely day
ain't it, Mr. Iceburg?

245
00:11:52.860 --> 00:11:55.700
Mmwell, that depends on
how the project's going.

246
00:11:55.730 --> 00:11:57.260
[CRAFTSMAN F]
We're actually
ahead of schedule, Sir!

247
00:11:57.400 --> 00:12:01.440
I guess Ice Pops is pretty
popular around here, huh?

248
00:12:01.480 --> 00:12:03.240
In a place where
skill is everything,

249
00:12:03.270 --> 00:12:05.140
it's only natural
that he shines.

250
00:12:05.180 --> 00:12:06.340
It's interesting.

251
00:12:06.380 --> 00:12:08.820
Although, shipbuilding has
always been a thriving business,

252
00:12:08.850 --> 00:12:10.420
before Iceburg's
time it was divided

253
00:12:10.470 --> 00:12:13.040
between seven
competing companies.

254
00:12:13.080 --> 00:12:14.340
Regardless, when he showed up

255
00:12:14.380 --> 00:12:15.910
there was something
about his talent

256
00:12:15.940 --> 00:12:18.150
that united
everyone in the city.

257
00:12:18.180 --> 00:12:19.420
With so many workers
flocking to him,

258
00:12:19.520 --> 00:12:22.590
it wasn't long before he made
the seven companies into one.

259
00:12:22.620 --> 00:12:25.520
And five years ago, Galley-La was born.

260
00:12:25.620 --> 00:12:28.080
His passion for shipbuilding
has never diminished

261
00:12:28.120 --> 00:12:32.160
and neither has the respect
that the craftsmen show for him.

262
00:12:32.190 --> 00:12:34.020
The people here take
pride in their work,

263
00:12:34.060 --> 00:12:35.560
so they don't
bow down to anyone.

264
00:12:35.650 --> 00:12:39.120
That goes for pirates
and Marine officers alike.

265
00:12:39.150 --> 00:12:43.220
That's the spirit this shipyard embodies.

266
00:12:43.240 --> 00:12:45.470
[LUFFY]
Hey buddy, I heard
you're a pretty good shipwright.

267
00:12:45.530 --> 00:12:47.300
How would ya feel
about joining my crew?

268
00:12:47.330 --> 00:12:48.360
Sound like fun?

269
00:12:48.400 --> 00:12:50.360
What the hell are you
trying to pull here, kid?!

270
00:12:50.380 --> 00:12:51.580
You insolent little wretch!

271
00:12:51.630 --> 00:12:54.040
Now, now, he was trying
to pay him a compliment!

272
00:12:54.070 --> 00:12:55.370
He doesn't know any better!

273
00:12:55.380 --> 00:12:56.640
Does this mean you
sailed all the way

274
00:12:56.640 --> 00:12:59.180
to Water Seven without any
shipwrights on your crew?

275
00:12:59.210 --> 00:13:01.340
Yeah, that's one of the reasons why we're
here.

276
00:13:01.380 --> 00:13:02.610
We need to have
our ship repaired,

277
00:13:02.640 --> 00:13:05.120
but we're looking for
a new crew member, too.

278
00:13:05.150 --> 00:13:07.650
We certainly have plenty
of shipwrights to go around

279
00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:09.150
but I wonder if any of
them would be willing

280
00:13:09.220 --> 00:13:10.620
to join a pirate ship.

281
00:13:10.650 --> 00:13:12.410
Mmwell, if you can find
someone who agrees

282
00:13:12.410 --> 00:13:14.350
to go with you,
I won't get in the way.

283
00:13:14.380 --> 00:13:15.320
You really mean it?

284
00:13:15.460 --> 00:13:17.490
Wow, I see why people like you.

285
00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:19.430
My offer still stands, you know.

286
00:13:19.460 --> 00:13:22.360
Mmwell I probably
shouldn't. I am the mayor.

287
00:13:22.400 --> 00:13:23.530
So what?

288
00:13:26.130 --> 00:13:28.440
By the way, Kalifa mentioned
earlier that Nico Robin

289
00:13:28.470 --> 00:13:30.550
was a member of your
crew... is that correct?

290
00:13:30.550 --> 00:13:31.580
Sure is!

291
00:13:31.620 --> 00:13:34.020
She's nice to have around since she's so
smart.

292
00:13:34.050 --> 00:13:36.120
Hahahaha!

293
00:13:36.150 --> 00:13:37.860
Huh? What's up?

294
00:13:37.890 --> 00:13:40.160
There's not something wrong with her,
is there?

295
00:13:40.190 --> 00:13:41.160
Oh, no.

296
00:13:41.190 --> 00:13:42.830
So anyway, it's okay

297
00:13:44.930 --> 00:13:47.570
I could watch them do this stuff all day!

298
00:13:48.570 --> 00:13:49.540
[CRAFTSMAN G]
Keep it steady!

299
00:13:49.570 --> 00:13:51.500
No need to rush, we got this!

300
00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:54.810
We gotta get a cannon like
that to put on the Merry!

301
00:13:54.840 --> 00:13:56.560
That'd be awesome.

302
00:13:56.560 --> 00:13:58.920
Whoa! It's so
shiny! And long!

303
00:13:58.970 --> 00:14:00.900
A real demi-culverin cannon.

304
00:14:00.940 --> 00:14:04.610
Wow. They've really
got everything! Hahaha!

305
00:14:04.640 --> 00:14:06.980
I wonder what it feels
like to fire one of those!

306
00:14:07.360 --> 00:14:08.570
Here's our chance.

307
00:14:09.740 --> 00:14:11.470
[USOPP]
Looks like it's made of copper.

308
00:14:11.500 --> 00:14:13.310
I bet it's super expensive.

309
00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:16.080
Ooh! I wonder if
they'd let me try it out.

310
00:14:16.190 --> 00:14:19.130
Oh, wait a sec. We've got
300 million berries.

311
00:14:19.160 --> 00:14:20.200
I almost forgot!

312
00:14:20.230 --> 00:14:22.560
We can afford at least one of those,
no problem!

313
00:14:22.600 --> 00:14:24.170
Maybe two or three!

314
00:14:24.200 --> 00:14:25.570
Ah!

315
00:14:30.840 --> 00:14:32.880
Hm?

316
00:14:32.910 --> 00:14:34.980
Did that guy with the nose leave already?

317
00:14:46.660 --> 00:14:49.490
[PAULIE]
Huh? Kaku...

318
00:14:49.530 --> 00:14:50.560
Where'd you run off to?

319
00:14:50.600 --> 00:14:51.960
An assessment.

320
00:14:52.000 --> 00:14:53.330
Wow, you are fast, huh?

321
00:14:53.330 --> 00:14:54.800
I take it you checked the ship?

322
00:14:54.840 --> 00:14:56.310
That I did, missy.

323
00:14:56.330 --> 00:14:57.870
Now that's a coincidence.

324
00:14:57.900 --> 00:14:59.340
We were wondering where you were.

325
00:14:59.340 --> 00:15:01.010
They told me why they're here.

326
00:15:01.040 --> 00:15:02.940
How was the ship?

327
00:15:02.980 --> 00:15:05.010
About that.

328
00:15:05.420 --> 00:15:08.840
Luffy! Get down here!

329
00:15:24.790 --> 00:15:26.620
What? You better not be lying.

330
00:15:26.660 --> 00:15:27.620
Cause if this is a joke,

331
00:15:27.660 --> 00:15:29.930
I'm sure as hell not laughing.

332
00:15:29.960 --> 00:15:31.960
I wouldn't lie about something like this.

333
00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:33.660
I'm a professional.

334
00:15:47.840 --> 00:15:50.680
Did something happen to Usopp?

335
00:15:50.710 --> 00:15:52.380
Well. He was looking
at the cannons

336
00:15:52.420 --> 00:15:54.820
over there a
second ago, but...

337
00:15:56.750 --> 00:15:58.790
Hey! You there!

338
00:15:58.830 --> 00:16:00.930
These bags are
yours, aren't they?

339
00:16:03.510 --> 00:16:06.580
Ugh... Usopp was supposed
to be taking care of those.

340
00:16:06.610 --> 00:16:07.650
But yeah, they're ours.

341
00:16:07.690 --> 00:16:09.030
Thanks, buddy!

342
00:16:09.060 --> 00:16:10.860
I can't believe that
idiot wandering off

343
00:16:10.950 --> 00:16:12.110
at a time like this.

344
00:16:12.150 --> 00:16:14.750
And how could he leave our
precious money alone like that?!

345
00:16:14.780 --> 00:16:15.980
Your money?

346
00:16:16.020 --> 00:16:19.620
Yeah, those bags you found have 200
million berries in them.

347
00:16:19.660 --> 00:16:23.050
[chuckles]
You're funny kid!
Ha! You wish! See ya!

348
00:16:23.050 --> 00:16:24.050
Hahah. That's a good one.

349
00:16:26.480 --> 00:16:29.520
So how much do you think
the repairs are gonna cost?

350
00:16:29.550 --> 00:16:31.360
We've got plenty
of money to spend.

351
00:16:31.390 --> 00:16:34.160
And no price is too
high for our Merry!

352
00:16:35.830 --> 00:16:36.730
And while you're at it.

353
00:16:36.760 --> 00:16:38.300
Why don't you make
her stronger, faster,

354
00:16:38.330 --> 00:16:42.170
as tough as nails and of
course decked out with cannons!

355
00:16:42.200 --> 00:16:43.500
I'd like some pretty engravings

356
00:16:43.540 --> 00:16:46.140
or some kind of
decoration along the hull.

357
00:16:46.170 --> 00:16:48.680
Also, can you guys renovate the inside?

358
00:16:48.680 --> 00:16:49.880
Not so fast.

359
00:16:51.350 --> 00:16:52.750
That ship of yours.

360
00:16:52.780 --> 00:16:53.890
She's gone on some really

361
00:16:53.920 --> 00:16:57.060
extraordinary
journeys, hasn't she?

362
00:16:57.090 --> 00:16:59.090
Yeah, you bet,
we've done it all!

363
00:16:59.120 --> 00:17:01.830
A while back we flew in the
sky, then fell back down.

364
00:17:01.860 --> 00:17:03.330
We climbed a big mountain, too!

365
00:17:03.360 --> 00:17:06.830
So, you gotta work hard to fix her up
extra good, okay?

366
00:17:07.430 --> 00:17:10.060
Her battle scars are far worse than you
think.

367
00:17:10.100 --> 00:17:11.760
Uhm... Then you're
saying it's gonna

368
00:17:11.800 --> 00:17:14.500
take a long time
for you to fix her?

369
00:17:14.530 --> 00:17:17.840
No, Ma'am.
It's not about the time.

370
00:17:17.870 --> 00:17:20.940
It's not about skill, either.

371
00:17:21.000 --> 00:17:24.270
I'm saying no one can
fix her. Not even us.

372
00:17:38.810 --> 00:17:41.020
So that means the keel's damaged,
huh?

373
00:17:41.070 --> 00:17:45.080
That's right. It looks like
it's taken a real wallop.

374
00:17:45.110 --> 00:17:46.310
No way.

375
00:17:46.340 --> 00:17:50.380
We... We've gone everywhere
without any problems until now!

376
00:17:50.420 --> 00:17:54.820
In theory, even if we
did everything we could.

377
00:17:54.830 --> 00:17:58.330
The probability of your ship
lasting 'til the next island...

378
00:18:01.400 --> 00:18:02.650
...is zero.

379
00:18:14.650 --> 00:18:16.650
Merry tell me.

380
00:18:16.690 --> 00:18:18.760
Is what he said true?

381
00:18:18.790 --> 00:18:20.620
You'll never sail again?

382
00:18:24.630 --> 00:18:27.070
This sucks.

383
00:18:27.100 --> 00:18:28.170
I'm sorry, Sanji.

384
00:18:28.200 --> 00:18:29.640
I was so focused
on the books

385
00:18:29.670 --> 00:18:31.840
that I didn't
notice when she left.

386
00:18:31.870 --> 00:18:34.270
There's nothing you should have to
apologize for.

387
00:18:34.310 --> 00:18:36.900
I just assumed that the
person I saw walking with Robin

388
00:18:36.900 --> 00:18:39.300
had to have been you
in your human form.

389
00:18:40.970 --> 00:18:41.940
Which begs the question:

390
00:18:41.980 --> 00:18:43.810
"Who was that masked man?

391
00:18:43.840 --> 00:18:46.950
And how were they able
to disappear so suddenly?"

392
00:18:46.980 --> 00:18:52.190
Yeah. Even Robin's
scent disappeared.

393
00:18:52.220 --> 00:18:56.220
I hope everything's
all right. I feel uneasy.

394
00:18:57.150 --> 00:18:58.950
Do you need an examination?

395
00:18:58.990 --> 00:19:00.890
No! That's not what I meant!

396
00:19:00.920 --> 00:19:05.560
It's what Aokiji said.
His words keep repeating.

397
00:19:05.590 --> 00:19:07.230
What did he say that worries you?

398
00:19:07.260 --> 00:19:10.700
It was all that terrible stuff he said
about Robin's past.

399
00:19:10.740 --> 00:19:13.850
And that very soon we'd
regret her being on our crew.

400
00:19:13.860 --> 00:19:16.900
Oh yeah, right. So do you
think what he said might

401
00:19:16.980 --> 00:19:19.280
have had something to do
with her disappearance?

402
00:19:19.330 --> 00:19:21.000
I don't know.

403
00:19:21.040 --> 00:19:23.340
But I know we're getting nowhere looking
for her.

404
00:19:23.370 --> 00:19:25.980
Let's head back to the ship for now.

405
00:19:26.010 --> 00:19:26.910
If nothing's wrong,

406
00:19:26.940 --> 00:19:28.980
she'll come back on
her own eventually.

407
00:19:29.010 --> 00:19:30.720
Yeah, you're right.

408
00:19:30.750 --> 00:19:32.620
[SANJI]
Hurry back to the Merry.

409
00:19:49.040 --> 00:19:52.210
What? You can't fix the
Going Merry? Why not?!

410
00:19:52.730 --> 00:19:54.200
I thought all of
you were supposed

411
00:19:54.230 --> 00:19:57.500
to be awesome shipwrights!

412
00:19:57.530 --> 00:19:58.740
Don't worry about the money!

413
00:19:58.770 --> 00:20:00.340
Look! We've got plenty!

414
00:20:00.370 --> 00:20:02.440
[KAKU]
It's not
about the money, son.

415
00:20:03.160 --> 00:20:04.570
No matter how much you pay.

416
00:20:04.600 --> 00:20:07.670
That ship can't be restored
to how she used to be.

417
00:20:08.570 --> 00:20:10.040
To be honest,

418
00:20:10.070 --> 00:20:11.240
I'm rather impressed
you were able

419
00:20:11.270 --> 00:20:14.110
to get that ol' girl this
far considering the condition

420
00:20:14.140 --> 00:20:15.340
that she's currently in.

421
00:20:15.380 --> 00:20:16.980
What's that supposed to mean?

422
00:20:17.010 --> 00:20:19.020
What exactly is going
on with the Merry?

423
00:20:19.050 --> 00:20:21.610
Do you know what the
"keel" is, Immoral Girl?

424
00:20:21.610 --> 00:20:23.510
The thing at the bottom of the boat?

425
00:20:23.550 --> 00:20:24.580
Yeah.

426
00:20:24.610 --> 00:20:27.450
It's the most important piece of lumber on
a ship.

427
00:20:27.480 --> 00:20:31.450
It crosses from stem to stern and provides
support.

428
00:20:31.490 --> 00:20:34.760
When you build a ship, you start off by
placing the keel.

429
00:20:35.000 --> 00:20:37.920
Then, you set the wood for
the stem, the stern, the frame,

430
00:20:38.160 --> 00:20:40.530
frame brackets, and deck beams.

431
00:20:40.560 --> 00:20:43.570
All of the pieces are
carefully assembled together,

432
00:20:43.600 --> 00:20:46.940
with the keel
serving as the core.

433
00:20:46.970 --> 00:20:48.870
It's the very
backbone, framework,

434
00:20:48.930 --> 00:20:51.000
and life of every
vessel constructed.

435
00:20:51.090 --> 00:20:54.530
And when it's severely damaged,
then that's the end of her.

436
00:20:54.590 --> 00:20:56.790
You can't just replace it with a new one.

437
00:20:56.820 --> 00:20:57.660
That would be the same thing

438
00:20:57.690 --> 00:21:01.160
as building a new
ship from scratch.

439
00:21:01.190 --> 00:21:04.960
That's the reason we, or anyone,
won't be able to fix her.

440
00:21:04.990 --> 00:21:08.530
Your ship's just a bunch of wood
stuck together awaiting her end.

441
00:21:08.560 --> 00:21:12.270
Hey, you! Don't say
such horrible things!

442
00:21:12.300 --> 00:21:16.540
Horrible or not, it's the truth.

443
00:21:16.570 --> 00:21:17.740
Okay, fine then!

444
00:21:17.780 --> 00:21:20.820
Just rebuild her from the ground up,
like you said before!

445
00:21:20.840 --> 00:21:22.610
Build another Going Merry!

446
00:21:22.640 --> 00:21:25.210
Coo coo! Sorry, not possible!

447
00:21:25.300 --> 00:21:26.630
Why not?

448
00:21:26.660 --> 00:21:28.960
We could build you
a very similar ship.

449
00:21:29.030 --> 00:21:32.200
But no one could build you one
that would be exactly the same.

450
00:21:32.230 --> 00:21:35.030
Nowhere in the world are there
two completely identical ships.

451
00:21:35.070 --> 00:21:36.670
It simply can't be done.

452
00:21:36.700 --> 00:21:37.870
What do you mean? Why?

453
00:21:37.900 --> 00:21:39.560
Tell me, are there
any trees in the world

454
00:21:39.560 --> 00:21:41.560
that grow exactly the same way?

455
00:21:41.700 --> 00:21:42.940
Huh?

456
00:21:42.970 --> 00:21:45.740
A sailing ship is mostly
built out of lumber.

457
00:21:45.770 --> 00:21:47.580
So the overall size
and shape of a vessel

458
00:21:47.580 --> 00:21:49.750
is determined by
the type of wood used.

459
00:21:49.780 --> 00:21:52.170
That's why an exact duplicate
of your ship can't be built

460
00:21:52.170 --> 00:21:55.840
by us or anyone, even when
using the same blueprints.

461
00:21:55.920 --> 00:21:58.760
If we made a ship like that,
with those specifications.

462
00:21:58.790 --> 00:22:00.660
The ones who would
notice the difference

463
00:22:00.700 --> 00:22:03.230
and feel that it's a
completely different ship

464
00:22:03.260 --> 00:22:07.100
would be all of
you guys! Coo coo!

465
00:22:07.140 --> 00:22:10.700
No way. So, you're saying
it's absolutely impossible

466
00:22:10.740 --> 00:22:13.580
for us to continue
sailing on the Going Merry?

467
00:22:13.610 --> 00:22:15.040
Unfortunately, yes.

468
00:22:15.050 --> 00:22:18.190
You can either wait
around for her to sink.

469
00:22:18.220 --> 00:22:22.100
Or you can dismantle
her immediately.

470
00:22:22.130 --> 00:22:23.800
[NAMI]
A real caravel!

471
00:22:23.830 --> 00:22:24.930
[LUFFY]
What a cool ship!

472
00:22:24.960 --> 00:22:29.040
Have you guys seen the front?
It's totally awesome!

473
00:22:29.080 --> 00:22:31.650
Okay! It's all done!

474
00:22:31.680 --> 00:22:32.550
Now the Going Merry

475
00:22:32.580 --> 00:22:36.820
has everything she
could possibly need!

476
00:22:36.850 --> 00:22:39.620
[ICEBURG]
Mmwell, your
ship is on her last legs.

477
00:22:39.660 --> 00:22:41.960
Why don't you look at this as an
opportunity?

478
00:22:42.000 --> 00:22:43.700
You happen to be in the
best place in the world

479
00:22:43.730 --> 00:22:44.900
to buy a new ship.

480
00:22:44.960 --> 00:22:45.860
You have money, right?

481
00:22:45.890 --> 00:22:49.160
I understand that
your ship was a caravel.

482
00:22:49.210 --> 00:22:51.740
Sailing on the Grand Line
in an old ship like that

483
00:22:51.840 --> 00:22:54.710
would be difficult
to begin with.

484
00:22:54.740 --> 00:22:56.980
No way. We're not
getting a new ship.

485
00:22:57.080 --> 00:22:58.410
[NAMI]
Luffy...

486
00:22:58.440 --> 00:23:00.850
The Merry is...

487
00:23:00.880 --> 00:23:03.750
The Going Merry is the ship
of the Straw Hat pirates!

488
00:23:03.780 --> 00:23:06.050
If she's repaired, then she can still
sail!

489
00:23:06.090 --> 00:23:07.780
She'll be fine! You'll see!

490
00:23:07.780 --> 00:23:09.810
She got us here today, didn't she?

491
00:23:09.920 --> 00:23:12.850
And you tell us out of nowhere she can't
sail anymore?

492
00:23:12.850 --> 00:23:14.920
I can't believe that! I won't!

493
00:23:14.950 --> 00:23:17.290
You guys are saying that
because you don't know her.

494
00:23:17.290 --> 00:23:21.060
You have no idea how strong she is!

495
00:23:21.090 --> 00:23:24.990
Then your plan is to keep sailing her
until she sinks?

496
00:23:25.030 --> 00:23:26.630
I'm shocked.

497
00:23:27.930 --> 00:23:31.730
So, this is the decision
of a ship's captain?!

498
00:23:32.480 --> 00:23:35.520
This discussion is over
for now. Just think about it.

499
00:23:35.960 --> 00:23:38.030
If you decide to buy a ship, let me know.

500
00:23:38.060 --> 00:23:39.300
My people can help.

501
00:23:39.330 --> 00:23:41.030
If you're able to
spend 300 million,

502
00:23:41.060 --> 00:23:45.500
we can build a state-of-the-art
ship for you. Kalifa!

503
00:23:45.540 --> 00:23:47.400
Here, if you'd look
this over, please.

504
00:23:47.440 --> 00:23:50.110
It's a catalogue of our
new and our used models.

505
00:23:50.140 --> 00:23:51.680
Use it as a price reference.

506
00:23:51.840 --> 00:23:53.310
Hey, Iceburg!

507
00:23:54.410 --> 00:23:57.220
There's someone at the front gate who
wants to see you.

508
00:23:57.160 --> 00:24:01.790
<b>Peepley Lulu
Pitch, Blacksmithing, and
 Block-And-Tackle Foreman
Dock One
Galley-La Company</b>

509
00:23:57.220 --> 00:24:00.050
It looks like those World
Government Officials again.

510
00:24:00.080 --> 00:24:02.020
Should I tell them you're unavailable?

511
00:24:02.050 --> 00:24:05.660
Mmwell, yes please.
Say I'm gone.

512
00:24:05.690 --> 00:24:09.200
By the way, Lulu. You've got
quite a case of bed-head.

513
00:24:09.230 --> 00:24:12.700
Oops! How embarrassing!
My apologies.

514
00:24:12.730 --> 00:24:13.930
Anyway, I'll ask them to leave.

515
00:24:13.970 --> 00:24:15.770
Uh, thanks! I appreciate it!

516
00:24:15.800 --> 00:24:18.000
[CORGY]
Hold it
right there, Iceburg!

517
00:24:20.900 --> 00:24:22.850
[CORGY]
Looks like
you're here after all!

518
00:24:23.140 --> 00:24:24.380
Hey! You two need to hide!

519
00:24:24.410 --> 00:24:25.980
Government Officials are here!

520
00:24:26.000 --> 00:24:26.940
What? Uh, okay.

521
00:24:33.220 --> 00:24:35.320
Mmwell, hello again, Corgy!

522
00:24:35.350 --> 00:24:36.990
As you can see, I'm not here today.

523
00:24:37.020 --> 00:24:38.490
[CORGY]
Yes, you are!

524
00:24:38.280 --> 00:24:41.850
Come now! I've come all the
way out here by Sea Train.

525
00:24:38.370 --> 00:24:45.040
<b>Corgi
World Government Official</b>

526
00:24:41.890 --> 00:24:46.920
Don't turn me away.
It's impolite!

527
00:24:46.960 --> 00:24:48.990
[sniffs]

528
00:24:52.130 --> 00:24:55.770
For some reason, I detect the scent of
pirates.

529
00:24:55.800 --> 00:24:58.740
Whatever, in any case,
I want to talk to you.

530
00:24:58.770 --> 00:25:01.410
I don't like you! Go away!

531
00:25:01.420 --> 00:25:02.420
Are you a child?

532
00:25:02.460 --> 00:25:04.460
Nuh-uh. I'm totally an adult.

533
00:25:06.840 --> 00:25:08.950
Then start acting like one.

534
00:25:08.960 --> 00:25:10.260
There's something of
special importance

535
00:25:10.300 --> 00:25:11.980
I need to discuss with you.

536
00:25:11.980 --> 00:25:14.680
I would appreciate it
if you would hear me out.

537
00:25:15.820 --> 00:25:21.020
Come, let's talk this over in private.

538
00:25:21.060 --> 00:25:23.700
Those Government guys
are so damn persistent.

539
00:25:23.780 --> 00:25:26.170
What the hell do they
want with Iceburg, anyway?

540
00:25:26.170 --> 00:25:28.310
Look, it has nothing to do with us.

541
00:25:28.340 --> 00:25:30.280
Probably political
stuff. Coo coo!

542
00:25:30.310 --> 00:25:31.980
[LULU]
Should I beat them up?

543
00:25:32.010 --> 00:25:40.120
I wouldn't, I mean they're
Government guys, after all.

544
00:25:40.290 --> 00:25:42.410
What? What's wrong?

545
00:25:42.410 --> 00:25:44.850
Something seemed
weird about these bags

546
00:25:44.880 --> 00:25:48.490
when I grabbed them.

547
00:25:48.490 --> 00:25:50.160
They're really light.

548
00:25:50.190 --> 00:25:52.700
Huh?
What do you mean?

549
00:25:54.160 --> 00:25:56.470
Oh, come on!
Stop kidding around!

550
00:25:56.500 --> 00:25:59.040
How could they be with all of that money
inside?

551
00:26:04.090 --> 00:26:05.100
Hey. Who's that?

552
00:26:05.130 --> 00:26:06.730
What's with the screaming?

553
00:26:08.160 --> 00:26:10.500
Idiots! What
the hell are you...

554
00:26:10.530 --> 00:26:14.340
Hey, lady! If you sit like
that, everyone can see your...

555
00:26:15.280 --> 00:26:18.550
[NAMI]
Don't tell me. There's no way!

556
00:26:20.510 --> 00:26:23.080
[LUFFY]
I don't wanna look!

557
00:26:25.020 --> 00:26:26.820
200 million!

558
00:26:26.820 --> 00:26:29.090
Gone! It's really gone!

559
00:26:29.090 --> 00:26:31.460
Wait! Look at these bags!
They're not even ours!

560
00:26:31.520 --> 00:26:32.320
[LUFFY and NAMI scream]

561
00:26:32.390 --> 00:26:33.220
[LUFFY]
Wh-Wh-What are we gonna do?

562
00:26:33.260 --> 00:26:37.830
Hey, hey! Keep it down!
What's wrong?

563
00:26:37.860 --> 00:26:40.400
Our money. Our money's gone!

564
00:26:40.490 --> 00:26:43.300
Vanished! Gone! Disappeared!

565
00:26:43.410 --> 00:26:44.350
[PAULIE]
What?!

566
00:26:44.380 --> 00:26:45.550
Paulie! Was it you again?!

567
00:26:45.580 --> 00:26:46.480
Huh? No!

568
00:26:46.520 --> 00:26:47.430
Honest?

569
00:26:47.430 --> 00:26:48.840
[PAULIE]
Why would I lie?

570
00:26:48.870 --> 00:26:50.140
[HATTORI]
You mean it?

571
00:26:49.870 --> 00:26:51.510
They're so noisy.

572
00:26:51.590 --> 00:26:53.290
Hey Kaku, question for ya?

573
00:26:53.320 --> 00:26:55.890
Why were you hanging out with
the Franky Family earlier?

574
00:26:55.920 --> 00:26:57.190
What are you talking about?

575
00:26:57.220 --> 00:26:58.950
I haven't been anywhere
near a single member

576
00:26:58.990 --> 00:27:01.020
of the Franky Family all day.

577
00:27:01.040 --> 00:27:02.840
That's strange. Huh.

578
00:27:02.900 --> 00:27:05.600
I was so certain that I saw your long
nose.

579
00:27:05.610 --> 00:27:08.050
Whoa, whoa, whoa!
Hold on a sec!

580
00:27:08.080 --> 00:27:09.380
That's Usopp!

581
00:27:09.390 --> 00:27:11.480
You saw him with
the Franky Family?!

582
00:27:11.800 --> 00:27:13.400
Well, you could
say he was with them.

583
00:27:13.470 --> 00:27:15.970
Or maybe you could say
he was being dragged away.

584
00:27:16.000 --> 00:27:19.510
That's called kidnapping!
Luffy! We gotta find him!

585
00:27:19.580 --> 00:27:21.080
[LUFFY]
Right!

586
00:27:21.110 --> 00:27:24.380
[NAMI]
Hey! Hold on!
Where are you going to look?

587
00:27:27.270 --> 00:27:30.140
Wait for me, Usopp!
Wait for me, money!

588
00:27:30.170 --> 00:27:32.170
I'm coming!

589
00:27:34.410 --> 00:27:37.010
Hey! Where can I find the
Franky Family's hideout?

590
00:27:37.230 --> 00:27:40.430
It's not really a hideout.
It's more like a workshop.

591
00:27:40.530 --> 00:27:41.700
From where your ship is docked,

592
00:27:41.730 --> 00:27:44.400
it's way up the seashore
to the northeast.

593
00:27:44.430 --> 00:27:47.370
It's off by itself.
You can't miss it.

594
00:27:47.400 --> 00:27:50.340
It's called the Franky House.

595
00:27:52.840 --> 00:27:57.760
[NAMI]
Luffy! Luffy!
What was he thinking?

596
00:27:59.840 --> 00:28:01.080
There's no way he
could find Usopp

597
00:28:01.110 --> 00:28:03.750
in this place all by himself.

598
00:28:03.780 --> 00:28:05.920
I can't keep looking for him right now.

599
00:28:05.950 --> 00:28:06.880
Before I do anything else,

600
00:28:06.920 --> 00:28:09.050
I need to take this
money back to the ship.

601
00:28:09.080 --> 00:28:11.250
If I march in there demanding
our 200 million back

602
00:28:11.370 --> 00:28:12.870
while holding 100 million,

603
00:28:12.910 --> 00:28:15.210
I'd be asking them to
steal that money, too.

604
00:28:15.210 --> 00:28:17.840
Yagara, please take
me to the bull shop!

605
00:28:17.880 --> 00:28:19.640
Shortest route possible!

606
00:28:25.600 --> 00:28:28.560
I'll take Zoro and the others
with me and teach them a lesson!

607
00:28:29.100 --> 00:28:30.670
Stealing from pirates?

608
00:28:30.700 --> 00:28:33.980
Franky Family, that makes
you the lowest of the low!

609
00:28:39.270 --> 00:28:42.570
[ELEVATOR GIRL A]
First Floor:
Backstreet Shopping District.

610
00:28:42.720 --> 00:28:44.690
Please proceed with caution.

611
00:28:44.720 --> 00:28:46.790
Thank you and
have a wonderful day.

612
00:28:50.220 --> 00:28:54.800
Hurry! I'm counting on you!

613
00:28:54.860 --> 00:28:56.760
What's her problem?

614
00:28:56.920 --> 00:28:59.190
Hey! Watch where you're going!

615
00:28:59.220 --> 00:29:00.760
I'm sorry, Sir!

616
00:29:01.960 --> 00:29:04.460
[thinking]
I hope Usopp was
able to escape from them...

617
00:29:04.500 --> 00:29:06.360
...somehow...

618
00:29:19.380 --> 00:29:20.410
[CITIZEN AA]
This is terrible!

619
00:29:20.440 --> 00:29:22.010
[CITIZEN II]
Where's this guy from?

620
00:29:22.030 --> 00:29:24.420
[CITIZEN U]
Hey! Does
anybody know him? Who is he?

621
00:29:24.420 --> 00:29:26.420
What's with them? Yagara, wait!

622
00:29:26.560 --> 00:29:28.870
Hold on a second! Stop! Stop!

623
00:29:34.700 --> 00:29:36.120
[NAMI]
Usopp!

624
00:29:38.280 --> 00:29:39.210
Usopp!

625
00:29:39.280 --> 00:29:41.180
Hey! Say something!

626
00:29:41.220 --> 00:29:44.180
Are you all right?
Open your eyes! Usopp!

627
00:29:44.450 --> 00:29:47.220
Hey! Usopp! Wake up!

628
00:29:47.250 --> 00:29:50.060
Excuse me. But are you
two pirates by any chance?

629
00:29:50.090 --> 00:29:54.330
Shut up! Stop staring
and mind your own business!

630
00:29:54.360 --> 00:29:56.860
Oh, no. Usopp,
who did this to you?

631
00:29:56.860 --> 00:29:58.830
Was it the Franky Family?

632
00:30:01.900 --> 00:30:06.840
It was. It happened
because I'm weak.

633
00:30:06.870 --> 00:30:10.810
They took... They took
all of the money.

634
00:30:12.200 --> 00:30:13.400
[USOPP]
Nami...

635
00:30:14.090 --> 00:30:15.890
Huh?

636
00:30:18.100 --> 00:30:23.200
How can I face the others now?

637
00:30:23.710 --> 00:30:28.820
We were finally
able to fix the Merry.

638
00:30:31.990 --> 00:30:35.510
I'm so ashamed.

639
00:30:37.710 --> 00:30:40.360
Dammit!

640
00:30:41.050 --> 00:30:43.900
Usopp, I understand.

641
00:30:44.440 --> 00:30:46.050
Don't worry about the money.

642
00:30:46.080 --> 00:30:48.980
We'll get it back together.

643
00:30:49.030 --> 00:30:50.570
[thinking]
He's been
beaten pretty badly.

644
00:30:50.600 --> 00:30:52.530
I probably shouldn't move him.

645
00:30:53.890 --> 00:30:56.560
[NAMI]
Look. Stay right here, okay?

646
00:30:56.590 --> 00:30:57.920
I'm gonna go back to the ship

647
00:30:57.960 --> 00:30:59.930
to get the others
to come and help.

648
00:31:00.070 --> 00:31:02.960
Chopper will have you fixed up good as new
in no time.

649
00:31:02.960 --> 00:31:05.370
And listen, don't worry about
the stolen money, all right?

650
00:31:05.400 --> 00:31:06.500
From what I've heard,
their hideout

651
00:31:06.520 --> 00:31:08.290
isn't too far away from here.

652
00:31:08.320 --> 00:31:12.820
We'll crush them and that'll
be the end of it. Okay?

653
00:31:12.820 --> 00:31:16.760
Hey! You think this is
some kind of show? Get lost!

654
00:31:16.790 --> 00:31:20.460
Stay put, okay? I'm going
to you some get help.

655
00:31:20.490 --> 00:31:22.030
I'll be right back!

656
00:31:27.230 --> 00:31:29.670
Hurry, Yagara! Go faster!

657
00:31:32.990 --> 00:31:35.580
[OLD GUY A]
A runaway bull!

658
00:31:37.980 --> 00:31:44.150
We were finally
able to fix the Merry.

659
00:31:44.180 --> 00:31:45.890
No matter how much you pay.

660
00:31:45.920 --> 00:31:49.160
That ship can't be restored
to how she used to be.

661
00:31:49.800 --> 00:31:53.470
[thinking]
Whether we decide
to give in and buy a new ship

662
00:31:53.470 --> 00:31:57.390
or we have someone else manage
to fix and improve the Merry.

663
00:31:57.630 --> 00:32:01.040
If we don't put the entire
300 million into this,

664
00:32:01.070 --> 00:32:05.510
our future journeys together
will be difficult, for sure.

665
00:32:05.540 --> 00:32:07.980
We need that money.
All of it!

666
00:32:07.990 --> 00:32:09.690
We have to get it back!

667
00:32:12.700 --> 00:32:14.280
What? Is that true?

668
00:32:14.280 --> 00:32:17.240
[ZORO]
Well, that's
what the shipwright said.

669
00:32:19.290 --> 00:32:20.580
This ship.

670
00:32:20.580 --> 00:32:23.500
It can't be fixed?
Even if we have money?

671
00:32:23.500 --> 00:32:26.460
Then... Then what's going
to happen to the Merry?

672
00:32:26.780 --> 00:32:27.780
Beats me.

673
00:32:27.820 --> 00:32:30.190
Honestly, it really
just depends on how Luffy

674
00:32:30.220 --> 00:32:33.460
and the others react after
hearing the assessment.

675
00:32:33.530 --> 00:32:35.570
Either way, I'm sure
they'll get it sorted out

676
00:32:35.600 --> 00:32:37.990
while they're
at the shipyard.

677
00:32:37.990 --> 00:32:41.030
It just seems so extreme!
I mean, look at her!

678
00:32:41.040 --> 00:32:43.160
Same old Merry that
she's always been.

679
00:32:43.160 --> 00:32:44.630
We've traveled
together on this ship

680
00:32:44.670 --> 00:32:48.940
all the way from the East Blue
to this place! Why now?

681
00:32:48.970 --> 00:32:51.340
You just answered your own question.

682
00:32:51.420 --> 00:32:55.160
Human beings become stronger
hardship after hardship.

683
00:32:55.220 --> 00:32:59.020
But ships are different.
Their wounds just pile up.

684
00:33:03.850 --> 00:33:05.540
[SANJI]
It's so hard to accept.

685
00:33:05.540 --> 00:33:09.100
And what is Usopp gonna say
when he hears about all of this?

686
00:33:09.440 --> 00:33:12.080
[CHOPPER]
I don't wanna
leave her! I love the Merry!

687
00:33:12.120 --> 00:33:15.090
I know, we all do.
But her wounds are too deep.

688
00:33:15.120 --> 00:33:17.400
There's nothing we can do.

689
00:33:17.400 --> 00:33:21.140
[SANJI]
The Merry's in trouble
and Robin's still missing.

690
00:33:22.560 --> 00:33:26.300
I'm worried about both of them.

691
00:33:26.330 --> 00:33:28.340
[NAMI]
Hey! Everyone!

692
00:33:30.450 --> 00:33:32.720
Look! It's Nami,
and she's alone.

693
00:33:32.750 --> 00:33:34.720
[SANJI]
Hmm?
That's weird.

694
00:33:35.100 --> 00:33:37.030
I wonder what happened to Luffy and Usopp.

695
00:33:37.520 --> 00:33:41.330
Hi, Nami!
Is everything all right?

696
00:33:42.870 --> 00:33:45.750
<b>Dismantlers</b>
<b>Water Seven
Outskirts
Northeast Coast</b>

697
00:33:45.750 --> 00:33:49.690
[FRANKY]
Ow! Ow! Ow! 200 million?!

698
00:33:45.830 --> 00:33:51.590
<b>The Franky House</b>

699
00:33:49.690 --> 00:33:51.590
[FRANKY FAMILY]
– Yes! 200 million!
– Yes! 200 million!

700
00:33:51.900 --> 00:33:54.870
What do you think, bro?
Awesome, right?

701
00:33:54.880 --> 00:33:58.090
[FRANKY]
Incredible!
You guys are amazing!

702
00:33:58.120 --> 00:34:00.560
Now that we have
this much money

703
00:34:00.590 --> 00:34:03.730
we'll be able to get
what we've always wanted!

704
00:34:03.760 --> 00:34:05.460
We actually did it, didn't we?

705
00:34:05.500 --> 00:34:06.660
After three years,

706
00:34:06.700 --> 00:34:09.230
our perseverance
has finally paid off!

707
00:34:09.260 --> 00:34:10.630
I'm stoked!

708
00:34:10.660 --> 00:34:11.460
[FRANKY]
Me, too!

709
00:34:11.490 --> 00:34:13.200
Every time we'd
have a little money

710
00:34:13.230 --> 00:34:15.430
we'd bet it all
on the Yagara races.

711
00:34:15.470 --> 00:34:17.440
And lose every time!

712
00:34:17.470 --> 00:34:19.410
Each time we'd collect
a criminal's bounty,

713
00:34:19.470 --> 00:34:20.780
we'd party and drink!

714
00:34:20.820 --> 00:34:22.520
And spend it all!

715
00:34:22.540 --> 00:34:23.780
We could never ever save up

716
00:34:23.810 --> 00:34:25.550
the 200 million
berries that we needed.

717
00:34:25.580 --> 00:34:29.620
Until now! We finally
have enough to get it done!

718
00:34:29.650 --> 00:34:32.460
[FRANKY]
And then some.
You guys did a hell of a job!

719
00:34:32.490 --> 00:34:34.320
[cheers]

720
00:34:34.390 --> 00:34:36.860
[FRANKY]
Now then.
I know it isn't much,

721
00:34:36.890 --> 00:34:40.630
but here's 5 million berries
to show you how grateful I am.

722
00:34:40.660 --> 00:34:45.700
Eat, drink, and have a blast!

723
00:34:46.300 --> 00:34:48.010
Hahaha! That's our bro!

724
00:34:48.010 --> 00:34:49.730
Party time!

725
00:34:57.010 --> 00:34:58.900
[RANDOM A]
What the hell?!

726
00:35:00.060 --> 00:35:01.630
Huh?

727
00:35:01.660 --> 00:35:03.000
What was that?!

728
00:35:03.030 --> 00:35:04.070
Show yourself!

729
00:35:04.100 --> 00:35:13.610
What fool would dare to
oppose the Franky Family?!

730
00:35:13.640 --> 00:35:16.780
Huh? It's that weakling
we ran into earlier!

731
00:35:16.810 --> 00:35:19.280
How did you manage
to even stand up?

732
00:35:22.560 --> 00:35:24.400
Give us our money back!

733
00:35:24.430 --> 00:35:27.270
[FRANKY]
"Give us our
money back," you say?

734
00:35:27.300 --> 00:35:31.540
Hmm, does that mean
you're one of the Straw Hats?

735
00:35:31.570 --> 00:35:33.540
That's right, bro!
He totally is!

736
00:35:33.600 --> 00:35:35.700
I was expecting all
of the Straw Hat crew

737
00:35:35.710 --> 00:35:37.740
to be really strong,
but this one's not at all!

738
00:35:37.780 --> 00:35:39.210
Hahahaha!

739
00:35:39.240 --> 00:35:41.650
Yeah, he's a complete
weakling! What a joke!

740
00:35:41.680 --> 00:35:43.880
Mind your own damn business!

741
00:35:46.400 --> 00:35:47.600
[FRANKY]
Whoops!

742
00:35:49.540 --> 00:35:51.440
[jeers]

743
00:35:51.480 --> 00:35:53.080
What's he doing?

744
00:35:53.090 --> 00:35:53.920
[FRANKY]
You've got some guts trying

745
00:35:53.950 --> 00:35:57.020
to take back the
money all by yourself.

746
00:35:57.060 --> 00:35:59.430
But I'm afraid I can't let you have it.

747
00:36:03.170 --> 00:36:09.010
Give it back!

748
00:36:09.030 --> 00:36:11.400
We need the money...

749
00:36:14.200 --> 00:36:20.910
...to fix the Merry!

750
00:36:20.910 --> 00:36:25.840
Her scars are deep.

751
00:36:25.840 --> 00:36:27.380
That's our money.

752
00:36:27.410 --> 00:36:30.350
It's to heal her wounds,
understand me?!

753
00:36:30.380 --> 00:36:32.920
You can't have it!

754
00:36:32.950 --> 00:36:36.120
Give it back to me right now!

755
00:36:38.790 --> 00:36:40.930
[FRANKY]
Hahahaha!

756
00:36:41.680 --> 00:36:43.630
What are you so worked up about?

757
00:36:44.530 --> 00:36:47.230
Seems like you love your ship quite a bit.

758
00:36:47.270 --> 00:36:49.840
[chuckles]

759
00:36:49.870 --> 00:36:53.440
No matter.
Whatever condition she's in,

760
00:36:53.470 --> 00:36:57.340
this money belongs
to us now. Understand?

761
00:36:57.380 --> 00:36:58.750
Damn you.

762
00:36:58.780 --> 00:37:00.650
[FRANKY]
Unfortunately, in this world,

763
00:37:00.650 --> 00:37:03.520
stealing from pirates isn't
really seen as a crime.

764
00:37:03.540 --> 00:37:05.380
Besides, this is
probably dirty money

765
00:37:05.410 --> 00:37:07.720
you swiped somewhere
along the way here.

766
00:37:07.750 --> 00:37:09.320
[chuckles]

767
00:37:09.400 --> 00:37:11.270
Just let us have it.

768
00:37:13.500 --> 00:37:16.370
[FRANKY]
Maybe you could
beg the Navy for help.

769
00:37:16.440 --> 00:37:18.460
Nah, they'd just arrest you.

770
00:37:18.460 --> 00:37:20.700
[chuckles]

771
00:37:20.730 --> 00:37:22.930
Too bad for Straw Hat Luffy.

772
00:37:23.030 --> 00:37:24.970
He's got himself a
useless crony who can't even

773
00:37:25.000 --> 00:37:27.940
handle the simple task
of guarding a little money.

774
00:37:27.970 --> 00:37:29.770
Such a shame.

775
00:37:37.450 --> 00:37:38.550
[FRANKY]
All right, boys!

776
00:37:38.580 --> 00:37:39.950
Show him all the hospitalities

777
00:37:39.980 --> 00:37:42.690
of The Franky House
while I'm gone.

778
00:37:42.720 --> 00:37:44.760
No time like the present
to start shopping

779
00:37:44.780 --> 00:37:46.610
with this pile of money.

780
00:37:46.650 --> 00:37:48.250
[chuckles]

781
00:37:48.280 --> 00:37:49.980
Man! This is gonna be great!

782
00:37:50.020 --> 00:37:53.690
Hold it! Special
Attack Exploding Star!

783
00:38:00.030 --> 00:38:01.160
[gasps]

784
00:38:01.200 --> 00:38:05.770
[FRANKY]
Remember my name,
long-nosed boy. It's Franky.

785
00:38:05.810 --> 00:38:07.920
If you wanna leave
this city alive,

786
00:38:07.950 --> 00:38:10.980
I'd suggest you
never cross me.

787
00:38:14.600 --> 00:38:18.940
Hey! What are you
guys doing?! Let go!

788
00:38:20.830 --> 00:38:22.640
Hahahaha!

789
00:38:22.670 --> 00:38:25.570
You call this little kid's toy a weapon?

790
00:38:25.600 --> 00:38:26.810
Hahahaha!

791
00:38:26.950 --> 00:38:29.280
[FRANKY]
See ya later, Long-Nose.

792
00:38:29.280 --> 00:38:32.790
Thanks again for the
200 million berries.

793
00:38:32.970 --> 00:38:35.340
[USOPP]
No! Wait! Give it back!

794
00:38:35.370 --> 00:38:36.770
Give back the Merry!

795
00:38:36.800 --> 00:38:40.010
You hear me?! Give it back!

796
00:38:40.040 --> 00:38:46.310
No! Give it back!

797
00:38:46.350 --> 00:38:51.550
[laughter]

798
00:39:00.060 --> 00:39:02.730
No! Give it back!

799
00:39:06.130 --> 00:39:08.000
No! Please!

800
00:39:10.870 --> 00:39:15.070
Give it back!

801
00:39:24.730 --> 00:39:27.090
[NAMI]
I need to wait here
until Robin comes back.

802
00:39:30.520 --> 00:39:32.260
If I march in with
the others right now,

803
00:39:32.290 --> 00:39:34.390
I'll just be in the way.

804
00:39:34.420 --> 00:39:36.130
It'll be scary, too.

805
00:39:37.720 --> 00:39:39.960
For now the best thing
I can do is stay here,

806
00:39:40.020 --> 00:39:42.130
guard the money and
protect the Merry.

807
00:39:42.160 --> 00:39:44.260
Don't let me down, guys.

808
00:39:44.300 --> 00:39:47.100
I'm counting on you!

809
00:39:47.200 --> 00:39:49.200
[SANJI]
Stop Stop!

810
00:39:53.090 --> 00:39:54.760
[SANJI]
If I remember correctly.

811
00:39:54.800 --> 00:39:57.100
This is the place
Nami was talking about.

812
00:39:59.690 --> 00:40:02.380
[NAMI]
Look. This is the
backstreet shopping district.

813
00:40:02.780 --> 00:40:06.050
You go up this canal,
turn, then here!

814
00:40:06.080 --> 00:40:07.890
Usopp is right here!

815
00:40:07.920 --> 00:40:09.090
[CHOPPER]
Got it.

816
00:40:09.120 --> 00:40:11.620
[SANJI]
Nami, where are those
dismantlers you mentioned?

817
00:40:11.660 --> 00:40:14.590
The Franky Family. Show me.

818
00:40:14.600 --> 00:40:16.000
[CHOPPER]
Hey, Usopp!

819
00:40:16.230 --> 00:40:17.100
[SANJI]
I guess he left.

820
00:40:17.200 --> 00:40:18.100
[ZORO]
Are you sure?

821
00:40:18.130 --> 00:40:19.740
Maybe you just screwed up the directions.

822
00:40:19.880 --> 00:40:22.050
[SANJI]
I'm not you, moron!
Shut your hole!

823
00:40:22.080 --> 00:40:24.050
[CHOPPER]
Usopp!

824
00:40:24.090 --> 00:40:24.920
What'd you say?

825
00:40:24.950 --> 00:40:26.120
Look at this!

826
00:40:29.500 --> 00:40:31.280
It's blood! See?

827
00:40:31.740 --> 00:40:32.700
That idiot,

828
00:40:32.740 --> 00:40:35.240
I guess he walked off somewhere on his
own.

829
00:40:35.270 --> 00:40:37.840
Wait, you don't think that he...

830
00:40:37.880 --> 00:40:39.280
Usopp, no.

831
00:40:40.880 --> 00:40:42.150
[LUFFY]
Aaaaaaaaaaaa...

832
00:40:42.180 --> 00:40:43.320
Huh?

833
00:40:55.870 --> 00:40:56.670
Luffy!

834
00:40:56.700 --> 00:41:00.070
Help! I'm drowning!

835
00:41:00.100 --> 00:41:02.810
[coughing]

836
00:41:04.010 --> 00:41:04.840
[SANJI]
What are you doing?

837
00:41:04.880 --> 00:41:07.480
And where the hell did you fall from?

838
00:41:09.080 --> 00:41:11.520
[LUFFY]
The shipyard. I was
trying to jump around

839
00:41:11.720 --> 00:41:14.820
like that carpenter
to look for Usopp.

840
00:41:14.860 --> 00:41:15.790
[CHOPPER]
Are you all right?

841
00:41:15.870 --> 00:41:17.010
[LUFFY]
Yeah.

842
00:41:17.040 --> 00:41:18.680
Oh, that's right!
I almost forgot!

843
00:41:18.730 --> 00:41:20.030
We've got a terrible problem!

844
00:41:20.060 --> 00:41:22.230
Usopp was abducted
and so was the money!

845
00:41:22.270 --> 00:41:24.770
We know. That's why
we're here. Come on.

846
00:41:24.800 --> 00:41:26.340
We were just about to
head over to the hideout

847
00:41:26.400 --> 00:41:28.070
of the guys that did it.

848
00:41:28.110 --> 00:41:31.110
I'll bet that's where we'll find Usopp and
the money.

849
00:41:33.980 --> 00:41:35.380
[SANJI]
Maybe he felt
responsible about what

850
00:41:35.410 --> 00:41:37.120
happened and went
to settle the matter

851
00:41:37.150 --> 00:41:39.820
with the Franky
Family on his own.

852
00:42:02.640 --> 00:42:04.570
[LUFFY]
Is he breathing, Chopper?

853
00:42:04.980 --> 00:42:09.250
[CHOPPER]
He's unconscious
but yes, he's breathing.

854
00:42:09.280 --> 00:42:11.750
He'll live. I can save him.

855
00:42:16.890 --> 00:42:19.190
You wait out here for us.

856
00:42:19.220 --> 00:42:20.720
'Kay, Usopp?

857
00:42:44.310 --> 00:42:45.080
[LUFFY]
We're gonna go level

858
00:42:45.120 --> 00:42:47.980
that stupid house
to the ground.
